Date: Fri, 8 Oct 1993 10:50:29 -0400
From: habs@panix.com (Harry S. Hawk)
Subject: Review: Eudora 2.0 

A few weeks ago I purchased the commerical version of Eudora (ver 2.0).
Eudora is a mail reader for the Macintosh. It is very popular and previous
versions have and remain in the public domain. 

This version has a variety of pricing options depending on how many you
want to buy. A single user copy costs $50. It comes with  a printed
manual, a printed quick reference guide, an a copy of MacTCP 2.0.2. and
a 800 number for support. It is sold by Qualcomm (800 2 eudora). 

If you were to buy MacTCP seperately it would cost about $50/60 so it is a
good deal. Eudora can be used on a Mac on a IP based network. It can also
be used to dial-in to a Unix host and comes with utilites to help that
"happen." I have been using Eudora on my vacation to dial-up and get my mail
once an hour or so. It does that automatically, and for the most part, has
been working well.

What makes it most interesting for Extropians and others who get
a large volume of mail is it's ability to filter messages. Based
on any header including to, cc:, from:, reply-to:, subject or 
body text, messages can be put into specific folders, have their
subject changed, or the priority of the message altered. Messages can
be filtered automatically as they come-in, go-out, or manually.

This feature has been very helpful to me. All my messages now
go to "right place."

What is missing is an easy way to sort the messages by subject order.

If you have a Mac you should consider getting this product. 

/hawk


--
