Date: Fri, 8 Oct 1993 16:12:51 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: Computing futures: the killer PDA

By now you've all seen the hype about the Apple Newton. You've read
the architecture reviews in Byte, marvelled at the size, seen the
Sharp clone, read that Matsushita, Sharp, and Sony have licensed the
technology, and taken a look at the real thing. And decided not to buy
one _just_ yet, until they get the bugs out of the OS, the handwriting
recognition works faster, the comms package is built in, and ...

I am going to buy a Newton.  Not today, not tomorrow, but soon.  I
don't have much doubt that the PDA concept is going to be _big_ -- as
big as the PC concept was in the 70's and 80's.  What interests me is
_where_ it's going to be big.

I'm a power-user, not a programmer. I run a personal newsfeed, work
with UNIX, and have a passing familiarity with half a dozen languages
-- but I try to avoid using all but English in day-to-day life. (I'm a
better prose mangler than programmer.) 

I'd like to paint a portrait for you of the kind of PDA I'd be willing
to spend money (as much as I had to!) to get hold of.

*       Architecture: it's already here. The Messagepad looks fine
        and dandy to me. Sure it needs a _lot_ more memory -- say
        10Mb of flash -- but it's got built-in data compression, and
        it doesn't have the overheads of a huge OS. Likewise, it 
        could do with a faster ARM implementation. But the ARM
        chipset, from what I know, is one of the cleanest RISC
        designs; and it's been in silicon for years, since Acorn
        launched the first Archimedes in about 87 or 88. I've got
        no complaints there.

*       Storage: inadequate at present, this is easily fixable. 
        The Messagepad (I am told) uses its internal dictionary
        to tokenize stored text; a compression ratio of 4:1 on text
        is usual. So a 10Mb flash card -- available today for about 
        $800 -- would give me 40Mb of data storage. Which is plenty
        for a pocketbook. As flash gets cheaper, capacity will go
        up. I expect to see high-end business PDA's have 100Mb as 
        standard within five to ten years.

        For a future direction, it's worth noting that the ARM 
        architecture comes with a macro-cell implementation (the ARM 
        600) that can be embedded within other chips as an on-chip 
        controller.  I'd like to see a 16 Mbit FLASH RAM chip with
        about 50% of the surface area given over to ARM cells, each
        occupying maybe 250,000 transistors. 8 cells is 2M trans-
        istors, the same order of magnitude as an i486. Each cell
        would address 2Mbits or 256Kb of memory. Now imagine the
        cells come with a small ROM mask containing a _fast_ regular
        expression or object search engine. Such a chip would make a 
        superb content-addressable filestore, being set up to locate
        information associatively in hardware.

        My ideal PDA would have no normal RAM as such, but a small
        (1-8) number of CAFS chips with a combined total of maybe
        8Mbytes (uncompressed) of storage. But that storage would
        be searchable with a latency measured in microseconds.
        The Newton already tries to do this in firmware; the wheel
        of reincarnation says that it's only a matter of time before
        they farm it out to dedicated peripherals -- the dawn of
        the smart PCMCIA memory card.

*       Input/output: is only half there. Handwriting recognition 
        is painfully slow, and so is handwriting. I write at 10-20
        words per minute max, and get cramp after fifteen
        minutes. I type at 40-80 wpm, and can keep it up for
        hours of burst-level activity. I talk at 120-180 wpm
        and can go on for days. Three guesses which input mode
        is most attractive?

        I'd like to see Apple integrate their PlainTalk
        technology into Newton, and leave the pen interface
        for editing. PlainTalk is the speech-to-text component
        of the new AV range of Macintoshes. With a bit of
        development, speech recognition will give us a 
        situation where you can dictate to your Newton (at up
        to 180 wpm -- faster than the fastest typist), then
        use the pen to cross out the "um"s and "ah"s. The
        standard copy-editor's mark-ups are simple to learn
        and would provide an excelent editing interface to
        allow users to correct dictation errors, and the
        combined speech/hand system would probably be faster
        than a keyboard. The result is that most users would
        relate to their Newton the way an old business type
        relates to their dictaphone: get the secretary to type
        up your letter, then correct it with a pen. Except that
        the secretary _is_ the dictaphone, and there's no time
        lag as you wait for the draft to arrive.

        This will, of course, require a big shift in our
        attitude to word-processing. But it's possible; the
        old "glass typewriter" model has slowly given way to
        the "desktop, scisors and pot of paste" editing
        paradigm, and this in turn may give way to a new, PDA
        oriented, "secretary/typist and editor with red pencil"
        paradigm. And I think this model is one that many people
        -- especially the technically illiterate -- will be much 
        more comfortable with.

*       Connectivity: barely there. Apple are promising email
        and fax services built into the next generation of
        Newton. Not good enough! I want a built in cellphone and
        modem. The speech-to-text will be used to input voice
        mail as well as text (you can edit the written
        rendition and let the PDA excise the verbal objects
        corresponding to the deleted words before sending the
        voicemail). Then there will need to be a TCP/IP stack, 
        and suitable power-apps to use it. My favourite is a 
        World-Wide Web client like Mosaic, only scriptable.
        Such a tool would be a knowbot development environment,
        able to access any inforesource on the internet and to
        obey your orders while you carry it around in your 
        pocket. You'd turn it loose in the background to
        hunt down new URL's, match their title fields against
        a list of your interests, and let you know what it
        thinks you might want to check out. Or you could use 
        it to work the Web, which may well be the main
        Internet user-interface paradigm by the time the net
        finally breaks into public awareness over the next
        couple of years.

*       Encryption: not there yet. (Okay you guys, who's porting PGP
        to the newton? ;-) What's more, it needs to be implicit:
        built into the OS from the ground up. Here, the object
        oriented nature of the Newton is a Big Win. Imagine a
        PGP external module, that plugs into the OS and encrypts
        _everything_ using your written signature (and maybe
        a spoken phrase) as a key ...

If this little lot -- especially the connectivity option described --
ships, I'd be willing to take out a mortgage to own such a brute. 
Today's toy would suddenly be a vital business tool. Indeed, it may be
impossible to do business without one.

I can't oversell the world wide web.  This is _vital_, or nearly so,
to the professional utility of a PDA.  I use WWW at work today.  Give
me a world wide web browser on the internet, and my customized home
page, and the name of a book -- any book.  Within an hour I will tell
you who wrote the book, where it was published, what its ISBN is, if
it's in print ...  and if you want I can even order it from a
specialist bookshop.  Give me a medical diagnosis and I will probably
be able to print you the abstracts or texts of most of the recent
articles on the condition.  (I did this one on the disease I picked
for my dissertation back when I was a pharmacist, and was astounded to
discover in two minutes more references than I had uncovered in two
weeks of searching -- including trips to the Science Reference Library
in London.)

What I want is a tool that weighs < 1lb, can store my entire
lifetime's writings, can send and receive email, can support a
scriptable interface to the world-wide web, contains a programmable IR
port (for controlling the stereo), functions as a voicemail system and
cellular telephone, can take dictation and print it out on any fax
machine, and that can securely encrypt information. I've wanted this
tool for years. I believe Newton is the first step towards it, and
while it isn't there yet we are immeasurably closer to it than we were
last month. 

Any comments?


-- Charlie







